-------------------------------------------------------------------------
BEFORE RELEASE: replace everything above with the licence you have chosen
-------------------------------------------------------------------------

This placeholder exists because lensa is being prepared for commercial sale
and the licence is a commercial decision, not an engineering one. Picking a
permissive open source licence on the author's behalf would be irreversible:
once a version ships under MIT or Apache-2.0, that version stays under those
terms forever, anyone may fork it, and anyone may resell it. The decision
therefore belongs to the copyright holder and has to be made deliberately.

Three things must happen together, in one commit, before the first public
release:

  1. This file is replaced with the real licence text.
  2. `license` (or `license-file`) in Cargo.toml is set to match. It currently
     says "MIT", which is wrong for every option below except the first, and
     is wrong today because no MIT text accompanies the crate. MIT's own terms
     require the notice to travel with the software.
  3. THIRD-PARTY.md is checked against the shipped dependency set, since some
     of the options below change what a recipient is owed.

The realistic options, and what each means for selling lensa:

  MIT, or the usual Rust dual MIT OR Apache-2.0
      Maximum adoption, zero friction, matches every dependency. But anyone
      may sell lensa, host it as a service, or bundle it into a competing
      product, with attribution as the only obligation. Revenue then has to
      come from something other than the code: a hosted service, support, or
      a brand. Choose this only if the plan is "open core, sell elsewhere".
      Apache-2.0 additionally grants an express patent licence and has the
      NOTICE mechanism, which is why it is the safer half of the usual dual.

  Business Source License 1.1 (BUSL-1.1)
      Source-available. Free for everything except a defined competing use
      (typically "offering lensa as a hosted recording service"), and each
      release converts to a named open source licence after a change date,
      usually four years. Preserves the ability to sell hosting while still
      letting users read, patch and self-host the code. Requires filling in
      three parameters: Licensor, Change Date, Change License, and an
      Additional Use Grant that says exactly what commercial use is allowed.
      Not an OSI-approved licence, so some corporate policies reject it.

  Elastic License 2.0 (ELv2) or the Functional Source License (FSL)
      Same intent as BUSL with less ceremony. ELv2 forbids providing the
      software to third parties as a managed service and forbids removing
      licence keys; FSL converts to Apache-2.0 after two years. Shorter to
      read than BUSL, which matters when the buyer is a small team.

  Proprietary / commercial EULA, with the source distributed to customers
      A per-seat or per-organisation licence with real terms: permitted use,
      redistribution ban, warranty, liability cap, term and termination. This
      is the option that maximises direct revenue per copy and the only one
      that supports a paid-binary model. It needs a lawyer, it needs a
      separate evaluation licence for anyone trialling lensa, and it means
      the crate cannot be published to crates.io.

  Dual: open source plus a commercial exception
      Ship under a copyleft licence (AGPL-3.0) so that anyone embedding lensa
      in a hosted product must open their own source, and sell a commercial
      licence to the companies that will not. Requires that the copyright
      holder owns or has CLA coverage for every contribution, which is easy
      today and gets harder with every outside pull request. Decide this
      before accepting the first contribution, not after.

Whichever is chosen: lensa does not bundle ffmpeg or Chromium, it discovers
them on the host at run time. That is what keeps ffmpeg's GPL/LGPL terms off
this repository, and it must stay true. See THIRD-PARTY.md.
